WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
 Welcome to Internet History, Technology, and Security.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:09.000
 My name is Charles Severance, and I'm a faculty member at the University of Michigan's School of Information.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.000
 I have been working on this course one way or another for nearly 20 years.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.000
 For me, it actually goes back to when the Internet first started.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.000
 That was in the mid-90s.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:27.000
 And in the mid-90s, I had the good fortune to have some friends that decided to help me go on television.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.000
 John Liskey, Amy Leahy, and Richard Wiggins.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.000
 And we had a television show.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:39.000
 First it was called Internet TCI, and then later it was called Nothing But Net, and then we called it North Coast Digital.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:50.000
 But it gave me an excuse in the mid-90s to make a bunch of videos and film folks and just ultimately create an interesting oral history of the Internet.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.000
 This is in the 90s, and we didn't even know how it was all going to turn out.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:01.000
 So we were walking around, Richard Wiggins and I were walking around and filming all kinds of things.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:07.000
 Another thing that I did in the late 90s was to build technology for teaching and learning on the Internet.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:14.000
 I hold the distinction of being one of the first people to stream an entire semester online.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.000
 I streamed it so early that I couldn't even stream video.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.000
 I could stream audio using real audio, and I had a set of slides that would flip every few minutes.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:32.000
 And you see a terrifying picture of me staring at you because there was no video in the upper left-hand corner of the screen.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.000
 I went from the Michigan State University to University of Michigan in 1999,

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:48.000
 and I rebuilt a new lecture capture system that captured cameras and document cameras and screen capture and allowed for annotation. I called that Clipboard 2000.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.000
 Everything back then was called Something 2000.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:02.000
 In 2010, I was part of a group that was going to convert I Triply Computer Magazine from Paper Magazine to a digital magazine.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.000
 And one of the ideas was we should have some videos.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:14.000
 So what I would do for about five years is add a few days to a business trip and take my camera and go somewhere.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:24.000
 I had two cameras and a little backpack and some lighting kit back in 2010, '11, '12, '13, and interview various people.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.000
 And that let me complete my set of interesting people in the Internet.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.000
 Some of the things that I did, I always sort of have a media bent to everything I do, is I would go around the country.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.000
 You can go to find these at drchuck.com/office.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:51.000
 And you can see 70 videos from all over the world of me meeting my students since 2013 when I first started teaching.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:57.000
 As I said, the idea is that we can't look at technology without understanding history.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:05.000
 And I want those with a liberal arts background to have a way into understanding technology because technology does not exist in a vacuum.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.000
 Technology has continued to change and is going to continue to change.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:15.000
 And if we don't take a look back, we will have no good idea about how to deal with the future.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.000
 And so we need to understand the past as we look toward the future.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:24.000
 So this material came from a course I taught from 2009 to 2017.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:29.000
 Most of the course was a Python course, but about halfway through, right after the midterm exam,

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:35.000
 I gave everybody a break from Python and taught a couple of weeks of Internet history, technology, and security.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:39.000
 And so that's what this course is. This course is the break from Python.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:45.000
 Now in my programming for everybody curriculum, I kind of think of this as the first course, the place that you start.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:50.000
 So the outline is starting with World War II going through 1999 as our history.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.000
 Then we look at the technology from the ARPANET to the Internet, TCP/IP, and all those protocols.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:04.000
 And then we take a look at the layer of security that makes all the kinds of commerce, et cetera, that we do on the Internet possible.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:11.000
 So we start back in the beginning. I think of the beginning of computation, communication.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.000
 That is the Internet and the technology we use on the Internet is going back to Bletchley Park.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:23.000
 So I love Bletchley Park. I've often met my students at Bletchley Park and I look forward to the next time I get to go to Bletchley Park.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:30.000
 So it's not practical for me to interact with the scalable class with hundreds, if not millions of students.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:38.000
 And some of my classes do have millions of students. I mean, I watch things, but Twitter is probably the best way to get me.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:44.000
 And I do want to know if something's broken. I don't. I don't. I want to fix things as fast as possible.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:51.000
 So just a little bit about me if you're still listening. I do have two tattoos. These are real tattoos.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:59.000
 On my left shoulder is my research and on my right shoulder is my education.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:02.000
 And I've got three degrees from Michigan State University in engineering.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:11.000
 And my faculty position is at the University of Michigan in the School of Library Science that's currently called the School of Information.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:21.000
 On my right shoulder is my research. And my research since 1997 has been in building and using educational technology to teach.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:28.000
 And all the logos are what I call the learning tools interoperability ring of compliance.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:36.000
 And LTI is how we plug in all of my auto graders, et cetera, into these teaching systems.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:45.000
 And so my research has been how to best teach with technology literally from 1995 until the present time.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:51.000
 I have a race car, which is one of my hobbies, and my other hobby is hockey.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:56.000
 And so that's a little bit about me. I have a number of folks to thank.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:03.000
 A lot of the material here was the work I did for IEEE Computer Magazine while I was a while I was a writer for IEEE Computer Magazine.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.000
 They've given me some permission to reuse some bits of it.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:15.000
 Richard Wiggins, my lifelong friend who passed away sadly, he has allowed me to use all of his video material.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:20.000
 And the copyright clearance is an important part of any free online course.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:23.000
 And Open Michigan helped me with all of that.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:36.000
 So with that, let's go ahead and get started.

